                - code: None
                - text: ", block until the future completes."
              - paragraph:
                - text: If a timeout occurs, it cancels
                - emphasis: aw
                - text: and raises
                - link [ref=f83e858]:
                  - /url: ../builtins/exceptions.html#TimeoutError
                  - code: TimeoutError
                - text: .
              - paragraph:
                - text: To prevent
                - emphasis: aw
                - text: from being cancelled, wrap it in
                - link [ref=f83e862]:
                  - /url: "#asyncio.shield"
                  - code: shield()
                - text: .
              - paragraph:
                - text: The function will wait until the future is actually cancelled, so the total wait time may exceed the
                - emphasis: timeout
                - text: . If an exception happens during cancellation, it is propagated.
              - paragraph:
                - text: If the wait is cancelled, the future
                - emphasis: aw
                - text: is also cancelled.
              - paragraph: "Example:"
              - generic: "async def eternity(): # Sleep for one hour await asyncio.sleep(3600) print('yay!') async def main(): # Wait for at most 1 second try: await asyncio.wait_for(eternity(), timeout=1.0) except TimeoutError: print('timeout!') asyncio.run(main()) # Expected output: # # timeout!"
              - paragraph:
                - text: "Changed in version 3.7: When"
                - emphasis: aw
                - text: is cancelled due to a timeout,
                - code: wait_for
                - text: waits for
                - emphasis: aw
                - text: to be cancelled. Previously, it raised
                - link [ref=f83e877]:
                  - /url: ../builtins/exceptions.html#TimeoutError
                  - code: TimeoutError
                - text: immediately.
              - paragraph:
                - text: "Changed in version 3.10: Removed the"
                - emphasis: loop
                - text: parameter.
              - paragraph:
                - text: "Changed in version 3.11: Raises"
                - link [ref=f83e884]:
                  - /url: ../builtins/exceptions.html#TimeoutError
                  - code: TimeoutError
                - text: instead of
                - link [ref=f83e886]:
                  - /url: asyncio-exceptions.html#asyncio.TimeoutError
                  - code: asyncio.TimeoutError
                - text: .
              - paragraph:
                - text: "Changed in version 3.12: Implemented using"
                - link [ref=f83e890]:
                  - /url: "#asyncio.timeout"
                  - code: asyncio.timeout()
                - text: ", a coroutine passed as"
                - emphasis: aw
                - text: is no longer wrapped in a
                - link [ref=f83e893]:
                  - /url: "#asyncio.Task"
                  - code: Task
                - text: when
                - emphasis: timeout
                - text: is positive.
        - generic:
          - heading [level=2] [ref=f83e897]:
            - link "Waiting primitives" [ref=f83e898]:
              - /url: "#id12"
          - generic:
            - term:
              - emphasis:
                - generic: async
              - generic: asyncio.
              - generic: wait
              - text: (
              - emphasis:
                - generic: aws
              - emphasis:
                - generic "Keyword-only parameters separator (PEP 3102)": "*"
              - emphasis:
                - generic: timeout
                - generic: =
                - generic: None
              - emphasis:
                - generic: return_when
                - generic: =
                - generic: ALL_COMPLETED
              - text: )
            - definition:
              - paragraph:
                - text: Run
                - link [ref=f83e920]:
                  - /url: asyncio-future.html#asyncio.Future
                  - code: Future
                - text: and
                - link [ref=f83e922]:
                  - /url: "#asyncio.Task"
                  - code: Task
                - text: instances in the
                - emphasis: aws
                - text: iterable concurrently and block until the condition specified by
                - emphasis: return_when
                - text: .
              - paragraph:
                - text: The
                - emphasis: aws
                - text: iterable must not be empty.
              - paragraph:
                - text: "Returns two sets of Tasks/Futures:"
                - code: (done, pending)
                - text: .
              - paragraph: "Usage:"
              - generic: done, pending = await asyncio.wait(aws)
              - paragraph:
                - emphasis: timeout
                - text: (a float or int), if specified, can be used to control the maximum number of seconds to wait before returning.
              - paragraph:
                - text: Note that this function does not raise
                - link [ref=f83e937]:
                  - /url: ../builtins/exceptions.html#TimeoutError
                  - code: TimeoutError
                - text: . Futures or Tasks that aren’t done when the timeout occurs are simply returned in the second set.
              - paragraph:
                - emphasis: return_when
                - text: "indicates when this function should return. It must be one of the following constants:"
              - table [ref=f83e942]:
                - rowgroup:
                  - row:
                    - columnheader [ref=f83e945]:
                      - paragraph: Constant
                    - columnheader [ref=f83e947]:
                      - paragraph: Description
                - rowgroup:
                  - row:
                    - cell "asyncio.FIRST_COMPLETED":
                      - generic:
                        - term:
                          - generic: asyncio.
                          - generic: FIRST_COMPLETED
                    - cell:
                      - paragraph: The function will return when any future finishes or is cancelled.
                  - row:
                    - cell "asyncio.FIRST_EXCEPTION":
                      - generic:
                        - term:
                          - generic: asyncio.
                          - generic: FIRST_EXCEPTION
                    - cell:
                      - paragraph:
                        - text: The function will return when any future finishes by raising an exception. If no future raises an exception then it is equivalent to
                        - link [ref=f83e966]:
                          - /url: "#asyncio.ALL_COMPLETED"
                          - code: ALL_COMPLETED
                        - text: .
                  - row:
                    - cell "asyncio.ALL_COMPLETED":
                      - generic:
                        - term:
                          - generic: asyncio.
                          - generic: ALL_COMPLETED
                    - cell:
                      - paragraph: The function will return when all futures finish or are cancelled.
              - paragraph:
                - text: Unlike
                - link [ref=f83e977]:
                  - /url: "#asyncio.wait_for"
                  - code: wait_for()
                - code: wait()
                - text: does not cancel the futures when a timeout occurs.
              - paragraph:
                - text: If
                - code: wait()
                - text: is cancelled, the futures in
                - emphasis: aws
                - text: are not cancelled and continue to run.
              - paragraph:
                - text: "Changed in version 3.10: Removed the"
                - emphasis: loop
                - text: parameter.
              - paragraph:
                - text: "Changed in version 3.11: Passing coroutine objects to"
                - code: wait()
                - text: directly is forbidden.
              - paragraph: "Changed in version 3.12: Added support for generators yielding tasks."
          - generic:
            - term:
              - generic: asyncio.
              - generic: as_completed
              - text: (
              - emphasis:
                - generic: aws
              - emphasis:
                - generic "Keyword-only parameters separator (PEP 3102)": "*"
              - emphasis:
                - generic: timeout
                - generic: =
                - generic: None
              - text: )
            - definition:
              - paragraph:
                - text: Run
                - link "awaitable objects" [ref=f83e1006]:
                  - /url: "#asyncio-awaitables"
                - text: in the
                - emphasis: aws
                - text: iterable concurrently. The returned object can be iterated to obtain the results of the awaitables as they finish.
              - paragraph:
                - text: The object returned by
                - code: as_completed()
                - text: can be iterated as an
                - link "asynchronous iterator" [ref=f83e1010]:
                  - /url: ../glossary.html#term-asynchronous-iterator
                - text: or a plain
                - link "iterator" [ref=f83e1011]:
                  - /url: ../glossary.html#term-iterator
                - text: ". When asynchronous iteration is used, the originally-supplied awaitables are yielded if they are tasks or futures. This makes it easy to correlate previously-scheduled tasks with their results. Example:"
              - generic: "ipv4_connect = create_task(open_connection(\"127.0.0.1\", 80)) ipv6_connect = create_task(open_connection(\"::1\", 80)) tasks = [ipv4_connect, ipv6_connect] async for earliest_connect in as_completed(tasks): # earliest_connect is done. The result can be obtained by # awaiting it or calling earliest_connect.result() reader, writer = await earliest_connect if earliest_connect is ipv6_connect: print(\"IPv6 connection established.\") else: print(\"IPv4 connection established.\")"
              - paragraph: During asynchronous iteration, implicitly-created tasks will be yielded for supplied awaitables that aren’t tasks or futures.
              - paragraph: "When used as a plain iterator, each iteration yields a new coroutine that returns the result or raises the exception of the next completed awaitable. This pattern is compatible with Python versions older than 3.13:"
              - generic: "ipv4_connect = create_task(open_connection(\"127.0.0.1\", 80)) ipv6_connect = create_task(open_connection(\"::1\", 80)) tasks = [ipv4_connect, ipv6_connect] for next_connect in as_completed(tasks): # next_connect is not one of the original task objects. It must be # awaited to obtain the result value or raise the exception of the # awaitable that finishes next. reader, writer = await next_connect"
              - paragraph:
                - text: A
                - link [ref=f83e1021]:
                  - /url: ../builtins/exceptions.html#TimeoutError
                  - code: TimeoutError
                - text: is raised if the timeout occurs before all awaitables are done. This is raised by the
                - code: async for
                - text: loop during asynchronous iteration or by the coroutines yielded during plain iteration.
              - paragraph:
                - code: as_completed()
                - text: "does not cancel the tasks running the supplied awaitables: if a timeout occurs or the iteration is cancelled, the remaining tasks continue to run."
              - paragraph:
                - text: "Changed in version 3.10: Removed the"
                - emphasis: loop
                - text: parameter.
              - paragraph:
                - text: "Deprecated since version 3.10: Deprecation warning is emitted if not all awaitable objects in the"
                - emphasis: aws
                - text: iterable are Future-like objects and there is no running event loop.
              - paragraph: "Changed in version 3.12: Added support for generators yielding tasks."
              - paragraph:
                - text: "Changed in version 3.13: The result can now be used as either an"
                - link "asynchronous iterator" [ref=f83e1036]:
                  - /url: ../glossary.html#term-asynchronous-iterator
                - text: or as a plain
                - link "iterator" [ref=f83e1037]:
                  - /url: ../glossary.html#term-iterator
                - text: (previously it was only a plain iterator).
        - generic:
          - heading [level=2] [ref=f83e1039]:
            - link "Running in threads" [ref=f83e1040]:
              - /url: "#id13"
          - generic:
            - term:
              - emphasis:
                - generic: async
              - generic: asyncio.
              - generic: to_thread
              - text: (
              - emphasis:
                - generic: func
              - emphasis:
                - generic "Positional-only parameter separator (PEP 570)": /
              - emphasis:
                - generic: "*"
                - generic: args
              - emphasis:
                - generic: "**"
                - generic: kwargs
              - text: )
            - definition:
              - paragraph:
                - text: Asynchronously run function
                - emphasis: func
                - text: in a separate thread.
              - paragraph:
                - text: Any *args and **kwargs supplied for this function are directly passed to
                - emphasis: func
                - text: . Also, the current
                - link [ref=f83e1063]:
                  - /url: contextvars.html#contextvars.Context
                  - code: contextvars.Context
                - text: is propagated, allowing context variables from the event loop thread to be accessed in the separate thread.
              - paragraph:
                - text: Return a coroutine that can be awaited to get the eventual result of
                - emphasis: func
                - text: .
              - paragraph: "This coroutine function is primarily intended to be used for executing IO-bound functions/methods that would otherwise block the event loop if they were run in the main thread. For example:"
              - generic: "def blocking_io(): print(f\"start blocking_io at {time.strftime('%X')}\") # Note that time.sleep() can be replaced with any blocking # IO-bound operation, such as file operations. time.sleep(1) print(f\"blocking_io complete at {time.strftime('%X')}\") async def main(): print(f\"started main at {time.strftime('%X')}\") await asyncio.gather( asyncio.to_thread(blocking_io), asyncio.sleep(1)) print(f\"finished main at {time.strftime('%X')}\") asyncio.run(main()) # Expected output: # # started main at 19:50:53 # start blocking_io at 19:50:53 # blocking_io complete at 19:50:54 # finished main at 19:50:54"
              - paragraph:
                - text: Directly calling
                - code: blocking_io()
                - text: in any coroutine would block the event loop for its duration, resulting in an additional 1 second of run time. Instead, by using
                - code: asyncio.to_thread()
                - text: ", we can run it in a separate thread without blocking the event loop."
              - generic:
                - paragraph: "Note:"
                - paragraph:
                  - text: Due to the
                  - link "GIL" [ref=f83e1077]:
                    - /url: ../glossary.html#term-GIL
                  - code: asyncio.to_thread()
                  - text: can typically only be used to make IO-bound functions non-blocking. However, for extension modules that release the GIL or alternative Python implementations that don’t have one,
                  - code: asyncio.to_thread()
                  - text: can also be used for CPU-bound functions.
              - paragraph: Added in version 3.9.
        - generic:
          - heading [level=2] [ref=f83e1083]:
            - link "Scheduling from other threads" [ref=f83e1084]:
              - /url: "#id14"
          - generic:
            - term:
              - generic: asyncio.
              - generic: run_coroutine_threadsafe
              - text: (
              - emphasis:
                - generic: coro
              - emphasis:
                - generic: loop
              - text: )
            - definition:
              - paragraph: Submit a coroutine to the given event loop. Thread-safe.
              - paragraph:
                - text: Return a
                - link [ref=f83e1096]:
                  - /url: concurrent.futures.html#concurrent.futures.Future
                  - code: concurrent.futures.Future
                - text: to wait for the result from another OS thread.
              - paragraph: "This function is meant to be called from a different OS thread than the one where the event loop is running. Example:"
              - generic: "def in_thread(loop: asyncio.AbstractEventLoop) -> None: # Run some blocking IO pathlib.Path(\"example.txt\").write_text(\"hello world\", encoding=\"utf8\") # Create a coroutine coro = asyncio.sleep(1, result=3) # Submit the coroutine to a given loop future = asyncio.run_coroutine_threadsafe(coro, loop) # Wait for the result with an optional timeout argument assert future.result(timeout=2) == 3 async def amain() -> None: # Get the running loop loop = asyncio.get_running_loop() # Run something in a thread await asyncio.to_thread(in_thread, loop)"
              - paragraph: "It’s also possible to run the other way around. Example:"
              - generic: "@contextlib.contextmanager def loop_in_thread() -> Generator[asyncio.AbstractEventLoop]: loop_fut = concurrent.futures.Future[asyncio.AbstractEventLoop]() stop_event = asyncio.Event() async def main() -> None: loop_fut.set_result(asyncio.get_running_loop()) await stop_event.wait() with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(1) as tpe: complete_fut = tpe.submit(asyncio.run, main()) for fut in concurrent.futures.as_completed((loop_fut, complete_fut)): if fut is loop_fut: loop = loop_fut.result() try: yield loop finally: loop.call_soon_threadsafe(stop_event.set) else: fut.result() # Create a loop in another thread with loop_in_thread() as loop: # Create a coroutine coro = asyncio.sleep(1, result=3) # Submit the coroutine to a given loop future = asyncio.run_coroutine_threadsafe(coro, loop) # Wait for the result with an optional timeout argument assert future.result(timeout=2) == 3"
              - paragraph: "If an exception is raised in the coroutine, the returned Future will be notified. It can also be used to cancel the task in the event loop:"
              - generic: "try: result = future.result(timeout) except TimeoutError: print('The coroutine took too long, cancelling the task...') future.cancel() except Exception as exc: print(f'The coroutine raised an exception: {exc!r}') else: print(f'The coroutine returned: {result!r}')"
              - paragraph:
                - text: See the
                - link "concurrency and multithreading" [ref=f83e1111]:
                  - /url: asyncio-dev.html#asyncio-multithreading
                - text: section of the documentation.
              - paragraph:
                - text: Unlike other asyncio functions this function requires the
                - emphasis: loop
                - text: argument to be passed explicitly.
              - paragraph: Added in version 3.5.1.
        - generic:
          - heading [level=2] [ref=f83e1117]:
